From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 18:43:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] DSpark adaptive verification: budget the drafts by a fixed
 linear step cost, nothing timed

The draft budget priced extra verification rows from cost curves each build timed
once at its first boot and pinned, scaled by adaptive_verification_cost_scale.
Equivalent builds then drafted differently from one timing sample (r6w drafted 3.8%
fewer tokens a step at sixteen streams than r6v, with identical outputs).

The budget now maximises expected tokens (sampling requests plus the admitted drafts'
survival probabilities, best first) over 1 + row_cost * rows: the cost model's own
argmax with its timed curves replaced by one recipe constant, a verification row's
cost as a fraction of a step's fixed cost (adaptive_verification_row_cost, default
0.02 from the profiled curves' shape). It is a rule of the inputs, the same on every
build and boot. The boot profile, the pinned curves and the cost scale are gone; the
eager shapes past the capture limit still run once at startup, untimed, so their
kernels compile there.

Christopher Owen's spark-ds41f vLLM patch 0184 (experiments/2026-10-18-r6x/vllm,
commit 03e8775c9f2c9a791bb49a2f4d97575020ec0f7b; the same three files as his
experiments/2026-10-18-dspark-linear-budget/overlay at 53ef355), rebased by the
dsv41-1017 recipe onto r6w with its scale-codes overlay, its patches 0001-0006
and his 25-replay profile overlay. The one conflict is the 25-replay profile
code in adaptive_verification.py, which this change deletes; the file is his
post-patch file byte for byte. model_runner.py keeps 0006's None guard on
get_mtp_target_hidden_states; everything else is his patch unchanged.

